# {{SITE_NAME}} — Agent Guide

This is a PokeDocs documentation site (Docusaurus distribution). You are
likely here to write or edit documentation. This file is the complete
orientation.

## Build & test

```bash
npm install
npx playwright install chromium   # once — mermaid renders via chromium at build time
npm run start                     # live dev server
npm run build                     # production build; FAILS on broken links or bad mermaid
npm run typecheck
```

Always run `npm run build` before committing doc changes — it is the
validity check for links and diagrams.

## Layout

```
docs/                 all documentation pages; sidebar mirrors this tree
src/pages/index.tsx   landing page — driven by customFields in docusaurus.config.ts
src/css/custom.css    site extras only; the color theme is compiled from config
static/img/           logo and other assets
docusaurus.config.ts  single source of truth: branding, landing cards, navbar
```

## Authoring conventions

- Every page starts with frontmatter containing a one-sentence
  `description:` — agents and search read it before the body. Add
  `sidebar_position:` to control ordering.
- Docs are served from the site root (`/`); a file at
  `docs/guides/setup.md` becomes `/guides/setup`.
- Link between pages with relative `.md` paths (`./authoring.md`), not
  URLs — the build rewrites and validates them.
- Mermaid fences are compiled to SVG at build time and the source is
  preserved in the HTML for agents. Write valid mermaid: a syntax error
  fails the build with file and line. Do not wrap diagrams in images or
  code blocks of another language.
- The color theme comes entirely from `branding.brandColor` in
  `docusaurus.config.ts`. Never hand-write `--ifm-color-primary`
  variables in custom.css.
- To exclude a page from a build, use `draft: true` frontmatter.

## Landing page

Edit `customFields.landing.cards` in `docusaurus.config.ts` — title,
description, and target route per card. Do not edit
`src/pages/index.tsx` for content changes; every card's `to:` must point
at an existing doc route or the build fails.
